  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe current approach relies heavily on local search from initial points, which may get trapped in suboptimal regions. We propose a hybrid optimization method combining global exploration and local refinement using a novel kernel-based initialization. The key idea is to generate solutions with local peaks (to preserve binary-like patterns) and smooth regions (to avoid discontinuities causing high overlaps). The algorithm will:\n- Use a kernel-based method to generate multiple candidates\n- Apply a two-phase optimization: global exploration followed by local refinement\n- Introduce a novel repair mechanism that ensures sum constraint while maintaining smooth transitions\nWe address the non-convex nature of the problem by combining derivative-free optimization with targeted local searches. The main advantages are better exploration of the search space and robustness to local optima.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nVariables:\n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n   : Step function values at n=70 points\n- Target sum: n/2 = 35\n- Objective: minimize C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n- Discretization: C\u2085 = max\u2096 np.correlate(h,1-h,'full')[k]*(2/n)\n\nRepair:\n- Repair any vector x to:\n  - Clip values to [0,1]\n  - Adjust sums by redistributing probability across sorted points\n- Ensure \u2211(rep(x)) = 35\n\nConstraints:\n- \u2200i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h[i] = 35\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n6. Validation and Return Contract",
